WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.905
iPhone backups and app data are central evidence sources.

2
00:00:03.905 --> 00:00:17.294
A backup can include application records, settings, media references, messages, browser data, and configuration information depending on backup type, encryption, iOS version, and device state.

3
00:00:17.294 --> 00:00:20.642
The backup is not simply a folder of obvious files.

4
00:00:20.642 --> 00:00:23.830
It has structure that must be parsed carefully.

5
00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:34.589
Backup manifests are useful at a high level because they help identify and map backup records, files, metadata, domains, and paths within the backup structure.

6
00:00:34.589 --> 00:00:41.443
A manifest may help connect an item in the backup to an application, service, or file path on the device.

7
00:00:41.443 --> 00:00:47.181
Analysts should treat this as a map into evidence, not as an interpretation by itself.

8
00:00:47.181 --> 00:00:51.644
App data containers often hold the most case-relevant artifacts.

9
00:00:51.644 --> 00:01:01.367
Applications may store SQLite databases, property lists, caches, thumbnails, media, downloaded files, preferences, and state information.

10
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:08.859
SQLite databases can contain structured records such as messages, history, settings, or application events.

11
00:01:08.859 --> 00:01:13.960
Property lists may store configuration, preferences, state, or metadata.

12
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.705
Both require context from the specific app and version.

13
00:01:17.705 --> 00:01:20.096
Deleted data has real limitations.

14
00:01:20.096 --> 00:01:29.580
Some remnants may remain in databases, caches, thumbnails, backups, or cloud services, while other data may be unavailable or overwritten.

15
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:39.782
Investigators should avoid claiming that absence proves nonexistence unless the acquisition method and artifact behavior support that conclusion.

16
00:01:39.782 --> 00:01:44.086
Deleted evidence should be reported with appropriate caution.

17
00:01:44.086 --> 00:01:49.266
File names, paths, timestamps, and metadata must be interpreted carefully.

18
00:01:49.266 --> 00:01:58.511
A thumbnail may show that a media file was processed, cached, or displayed by a system component, but it may not prove intentional viewing.

19
00:01:58.511 --> 00:02:05.365
A timestamp may reflect creation, modification, backup, sync, import, or database update behavior.

20
00:02:05.365 --> 00:02:12.777
Backup and app artifacts become meaningful when they are parsed in context and correlated with other records.
